Thanks. Both are posted as "[PATCH v8 0/6] jails/instances/lab-mail:
Build the mail relay jail" [1]. Patch 1 is the spec with the rename,
and patches 2 to 6 build lab-mail and point lab-claude's clients at
it.

The implementation now follows the spec. The earlier posting read
the login and password from two files and put them in smtprelay's
URL unescaped, so a password with "@", ":", "/" or "#" would have
broken it. Now smtprelay and mbsync both read the one mail-creds
line, split at the first space, and a small script percent-encodes
both halves for smtprelay. The cover letter has the interdiffs.

None of the services has run yet. smtprelay, mbsync, msmtp and
Dovecot are not installed in lab-claude, and I cannot build a jail
from here, so the spec's acceptance test needs to run on beastie.
I tested the encoding script and the mbsync split on a line with
reserved characters, a space, and UTF-8 in the password. That
smtprelay decodes the escapes is inference from Go's net/url; I have
not run it.

To set up the secret on beastie:

    printf '%s %s\n' 'login@example.com' 'app-password' \
        > /jails/data/lab-mail/secrets/mail-creds

The README in patch 2 has the ownership and modes.

